TITLE:: BlipSync
SUMMARY:: Band-limited harmonic-band oscillator with phase I/O and sub-sample hard sync
CATEGORIES:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
RELATED:: Classes/Blip, Classes/Saw, Classes/Pulse, Classes/Phasor

DESCRIPTION::

A band-limited impulse train in the spirit of link::Classes/Blip::, rebuilt around
four changes:

LIST::
## The band is specified in STRONG::Hz::, not as a harmonic count, so the spectral
width does not follow the fundamental around.
## The band edges are STRONG::fractional::. A harmonic fades in or out linearly
instead of switching on or off, so sweeping any parameter produces no stepping,
no crossfade transient and no zipper noise.
## Every input is read STRONG::per sample::, so audio-rate FM and PM behave
correctly. Blip samples its frequency input once per control block.
## Phase is both an STRONG::input and an output::, and there is a sub-sample
accurate hard sync input with polyBLEP step correction.
::

BlipSync returns TWO channels: code::[ waveform, phase ]::. The phase output is a
0..1 ramp in cycles, intended for coupling, sync chains and analysis -- it is a
raw sawtooth and is not band-limited, so do not listen to it directly.

STRONG::How it works::

The output is the closed form of a weighted cosine sum

CODE::
y(p) = (1/W) * sum_k  w_k * cos(2*pi*k*p)
w_k  = clip(maxfreq/freq - k + 1, 0, 1) * clip(k - minfreq/freq + 1, 0, 1)
::

evaluated as a shifted Dirichlet kernel. The 0/0 singularity that sits exactly on
the impulse peak is removed analytically rather than guarded against, so there is
no reciprocal-sine table and no interpolation error: the waveform is accurate to
about -218 dB, against about -51 dB for Blip.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: ar

ARGUMENT:: freq
Fundamental frequency in Hz. Audio or control rate. Negative values run the
oscillator backwards. Default 440.

ARGUMENT:: maxfreq
Top of the harmonic band in Hz -- the band limit. Harmonics are included up to
this frequency, with the topmost one faded in fractionally. Internally clamped to
just under Nyquist; when STRONG::freq:: itself rises past the limit the
oscillator fades to silence instead of folding over. Default 20000.

ARGUMENT:: minfreq
Bottom of the harmonic band in Hz. 0 (the default) means the band starts at the
fundamental, i.e. an ordinary impulse train. Raising it turns the oscillator into
a band of harmonics -- a formant-like burst whose centre and width you dial in Hz,
independently of pitch.

ARGUMENT:: phase
Phase offset in CYCLES (0..1), NOT radians. Added to the running phase rather
than accumulated into it, so an audio-rate signal here is phase modulation. Note
that the phase OUTPUT includes this offset. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: sync
Sync input. See STRONG::syncMode::. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: syncPhase
Phase, in cycles, to jump to when sync fires. Nonzero values give the classic
offset hard-sync timbres. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: syncMode
INIT RATE. code::0:: -- STRONG::sync:: is a trigger and fires on a rising
crossing of zero; the crossing time is interpolated from the two samples either
side. code::1:: -- STRONG::sync:: is a 0..1 phase ramp (another BlipSync's phase
output, link::Classes/Phasor::, code::LFSaw.ar(f).range(0,1)::) and fires on the
wrap. Mode 1 recovers the crossing time exactly and is the accurate way to chain
oscillators. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: iphase
INIT RATE. Initial phase in cycles. Use it to spread the starting phases of an
oscillator bank. Default 0.

ARGUMENT:: normalize
INIT RATE. code::0:: -- peak normalisation, the waveform peaks at 1.0 like Blip;
narrow bands are therefore quiet. code::1:: -- RMS normalisation, roughly
constant loudness as the band width changes, which is usually what you want when
sweeping STRONG::maxfreq::. Default 0.

METHOD:: arSig
As code::ar::, but returns only the waveform channel and accepts
code::mul:: / code::add::.

EXAMPLES::

CODE::
s.boot;

// 1. the basic thing: bandwidth in Hz, so timbre stays put across the keyboard
(
{
    var f = MouseX.kr(50, 800, 1);
    BlipSync.ar(f, 4000).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// compare with Blip, where a fixed harmonic count means the brightness
// tracks the pitch and the harmonics audibly step in and out
(
{
    var f = MouseX.kr(50, 800, 1);
    Blip.ar(f, 20) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 2. sweeping the band limit: no stepping, no zipper
(
{
    var top = SinOsc.kr(0.2).exprange(300, 16000);
    BlipSync.ar(110, top, normalize: 1).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 3. minfreq turns it into a moving band of harmonics (formant-ish)
(
{
    var centre = SinOsc.kr(0.13).exprange(300, 5000);
    var width  = 800;
    BlipSync.ar(80, centre + width, (centre - width).max(0), normalize: 1).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 4. audio-rate FM actually works here
(
{
    var mod = SinOsc.ar(XLine.kr(1, 800, 8)) * 200;
    BlipSync.ar(220 + mod, 8000).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 5. hard sync from a phase-ramp master (syncMode 1 -- sample-exact)
(
{
    var master = Phasor.ar(0, 110 * SampleDur.ir, 0, 1);
    var slave  = BlipSync.ar(
        freq: MouseX.kr(110, 2200, 1),
        maxfreq: 8000,
        sync: master,
        syncMode: 1
    );
    slave[0] * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 6. phase output -> Kuramoto-style coupling between two oscillators.
// Each oscillator pulls its frequency toward the other's phase.
(
{
    var k = MouseY.kr(0, 60);
    var fb = LocalIn.ar(2);                 // the two phase outputs, 0..1
    var d  = sin(2pi * (fb[1] - fb[0]));    // phase difference
    var a  = BlipSync.ar(200 + (k * d),      6000, iphase: 0.0);
    var b  = BlipSync.ar(203 - (k * d),      6000, iphase: 0.37);
    LocalOut.ar([a[1], b[1]]);
    (a[0] + b[0]) * 0.15 ! 2
}.play
)

// 7. phase offset as an audio-rate input = phase modulation
(
{
    var pm = SinOsc.ar(220) * MouseX.kr(0, 1);
    BlipSync.ar(220, 6000, phase: pm).at(0) * 0.2 ! 2
}.play
)

// 8. an oscillator bank with spread initial phases
(
{
    var n = 8;
    Mix.fill(n, { |i|
        BlipSync.ar(100 * (i + 1), 12000, iphase: i / n).at(0)
    }) * 0.05 ! 2
}.play
)
::

STRONG::Notes::

LIST::
## Hard sync responds one sample after the detected edge: the crossing is found
between two samples, and the polyBLEP correction can only be scheduled forward.
There is no output latency.
## Hard sync is the only remaining aliasing source, and it is inherent -- a step
discontinuity has infinite bandwidth. The correction puts it around -28 dB
relative to the signal, against -19 dB for an uncorrected reset. Lowering
STRONG::maxfreq:: reduces it further.
## The fractional top edge sits one harmonic above STRONG::maxfreq::. If that
would cross Nyquist it is dropped, which reintroduces stepping at the very top.
Keep STRONG::maxfreq:: at least one harmonic spacing below Nyquist -- the 20000
default does so for any fundamental under about 4 kHz.
## A fundamental above the band limit fades out rather than folding over.
## The band limit applies to the UNMODULATED spectrum. Deep FM or PM spreads each
harmonic well past STRONG::maxfreq:: and will alias; lower STRONG::maxfreq:: when
modulating hard.
## code::normalize: 1:: holds the RMS at 0.707 for any bandwidth, so the peak is
sqrt(W) -- about 7.4 for a 54-harmonic band. Scale accordingly.
## code::ar:: has no mul/add because it returns two channels and madd would scale
the phase output too. Use code::.at(0) * amp:: or link::#*arSig::.
## CPU is about 0.10% of one core per voice with control-rate parameters, 0.21%
when freq, minfreq or maxfreq is audio rate -- independent of how many harmonics
the band contains.
::
